# $name

An experiment built on [alhazen](https://github.com/sh4r11f/alhazen).

## Getting started

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"     # no renderer needed for this
pytest                      # the task's own tests, on a fake clock
python run.py --mode simulate --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --headless
```

That last command runs a complete session with nobody in the chair and no
window open, and writes a real run directory under `data-rehearsal/` —
trials, events and frame timings, a config snapshot, a session log and a
manifest — in exactly the layout a real session writes under `data/`.

`run.py` starts the experiment in any of alhazen's six modes (`--mode demo`,
`movie`, `measure`, `simulate`, `test`, `run`), and every mode runs on every
rig file:

```bash
python run.py --mode demo     --rig configs/rig-mac.yaml              # look at the stimulus
python run.py --mode test     --rig configs/rig-mac.yaml --sub dev --ses 1   # play it, mouse as gaze
python run.py --mode simulate --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --seed 1     # watch it run itself, on the rig
python run.py --mode movie    --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --out movies # files, at the rig's sizes
```

A rig file describes a machine, not a purpose. Simulate mode stands the
rig's real devices down (the autopilot supplies gaze; the pump and the sync
lines are logged, not fired) and prints what it stood down before trial
one; `--headless` takes the window away too. Test mode, on a machine with no
tracker, takes the mouse cursor as gaze — `--mouse` asks for that on a rig
whose tracker is off.

## What is where

| | |
|---|---|
| `src/$package/task.py` | the experiment: parameters, events, outcomes, and what a trial is made of |
| `configs/task.yaml` | the task's parameters for a session |
| `configs/rig-mac.yaml` | a development laptop: a window, no devices — read its Retina notes before judging a stimulus |
| `configs/rig-lab.yaml` | the rig: fill in your monitor's real geometry and uncomment the devices it has |
| `tests/` | the task's tests, using `alhazen.testing`'s doubles |

Both rig files carry starting-point monitor numbers, not measurements. The
numbers decide what a degree of visual angle is, so measure them for each
machine (`alhazen calibrate ruler` checks them with a tape measure) — that
is why there is one rig file per machine.

## On the rig

```bash
alhazen check-rig --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --pulse   # before the subject arrives
alhazen run --task $task_name --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --sub s01 --ses 1
```

`check-rig` constructs the same device backends a session would, so a clean
check predicts a working session. `--pulse` fires one reward pulse and one
pulse per sync line, so you can hear the pump and see the lines.

The lab config enables the live browser dashboard. It updates after each
trial but remains read-only while the task is running. Press `P` on the
experimenter keyboard before using Resume, Calibrate, Give reward, Quit, or
training-stage controls in the browser; this prevents browser focus from
interfering with an active subject trial.

## Afterwards

```bash
alhazen report --run data/sub-s01/ses-001/run-01_task-$task_name
```

Trial counts by outcome, frame-drop statistics, and a manifest check. With
`--neural <recording dir>` it also aligns the session's clock to the
recording's and measures the display's latency from the photodiode.
